# Coach speech: the appraisal after the game (src/coach/appraisal.cpp). German.
# Every language has exactly the English keys (and the same variants). Read the header of
# en/appraisal.lang for the placeholders. The appraisal is always positive and never blames.
#
# German notes:
# - {text} inserts a phase (appraisal.phase.*: nominative with its article, "die Eröffnung",
#   "das Mittelspiel", "das Endspiel"; feminine and neuter, so nominative = accusative and the
#   sentences use them only in those cases) or a draw reason (appraisal.reason.*: a prepositional
#   phrase that fits "Remis {text}." and "Die Partie endet {text} remis.").
# - {theme} is an infinitive phrase or a noun phrase without a full stop: every sentence puts it
#   after a colon ("Eine Sache zum Üben: auf Doppelangriffe achten.").
# - {level} is a level name as in the menus; the sentences say "die Stufe {level}" so the name
#   never needs a case ending.
# - {opening:def} = the opening name with its article, always in the nominative here (some names
#   are masculine: "der Torre-Angriff").
# - Move numbers: "bei Zug {n}" (German ordinals are not spoken). Counts before a noun use the count
#   forms of common.lang ({moves:move_dat} "in vierzig Zügen", {acc:percent} "ein Prozent").
#   No {x:time} starts a sentence (the written "einmal" would stay lowercase).

